"""P8 DL-TRAINING-PATH (owner, 2026-10-06): feed P4/P5 from the S1 production teacher ledger, incrementally.

The S1-RAW-NOW production run (RUN/receipts/trainonly-v2/s1-prod, written by its own code/ledger.py and
code/s1prod_context.py; this module never writes there) selects one GP teacher per v2 context on inner CV only. This
module tails its append-only ledgers and turns every FINALIZED context into one create-only, P5-compatible shard that
dltrain.sampler.Corpus.build consumes, so the PolicyLM trainer can start as soon as S1 labels exist.

SOURCES (read-only; the production formats are adapted in exactly three small functions, see "ASSUMED FORMATS"):
- LEDGER_DIR/ledger.header.json (jope.dl-s1-prod-ledger-header/v1): pins the ledgers, the input list, P3 and the
  denylist. Its sha256 is bound into OUT/header.json; a different header refuses.
- the ledgers named by header["ledgers"] (production, research): append-only JSONL, tailed with a persisted cursor
  {path, offset, prefix_sha256, lines}. Every poll re-hashes the consumed prefix: a rewrite or truncation refuses loudly
  (LedgerRewritten). A torn last line (no newline) is left for the next poll. Rows: open, assign, defer, release, final.
- a context is FINALIZED only by its `final` row (ledger.py collect); assigned-but-not-final contexts are pending.
  A second, identical final row is a counted duplicate; a different one is a conflict (conflicts.jsonl, CLI exit 3,
  and the loader drops the context).
- LEDGER_DIR/contexts/<ctx>/{final.json, summary.tar.gz} (collected, create-only): checked against the final row,
  the header and each other; summary holds attempts.jsonl (reserve/finish per ordinal) and digests.json (sha256 of
  every file of the context directory on the execution host).
- the per-attempt evaluation records (g0-evaluations.json, genNN/KK.pack.json.gz) stay on the execution host. They are
  read from local mirrors (--evaluations-root DIR/<ctx>/...) or, opt-in, streamed read-only over ssh from the final
  row's host_custody (`tar -cf -` of exactly the needed names; nothing is stored). Every file must match digests.json.
- the context's teacher JTT1 and context.json (input-list row, sha256-pinned): y_train = the outer-TRAIN targets only,
  decoded with dltrain.jtt1/corpus_schema.train_input (bytes at or beyond the test offset are never decoded).
- P3 holdout.json + families registry (paths and sha256 from the ledger header) and the denylist: admission.

OUTER-TEST PATH DENY (owner rule relayed 2026-10-06T11:1xZ). S1 outer-test outputs live under s1-prod/outer-test/
(tagged unreviewed_adapter). Every file this module or its loader opens passes guard_path() first
(tranche.assert_label_path: the lexical, absolute and resolved forms must not contain s1-prod/outer-test, so symlinks
into it are refused too); globs skip such paths, and no directory of the ledger dir is ever walked.

LEAK RULE (owner, unchanged). Labels come from inner-CV selection products only: inner_cv[j].metrics.rmse,
inner_cv[j].baseline_metrics.rmse and inner_cv[j].wall_us of jope.s1-evaluation/v1 records with kind == "inner_cv".
No outer-test stream is ever opened (only the files named above are read). A selection record that carries any
outer-test field (a key token test/official/outer/holdout/heldout with a non-null value, or a fold of another kind)
REFUSES its context; a final row or final.json carrying a numeric outer-test value refuses too. Nothing derived from
the outer-test rows reaches a shard: it holds y_train, train_rows_sha256 and inner-CV facts only, while input
identities (teacher, final, summary, ledger line) go to a separate provenance sidecar.
A context whose outer-train features hold an exact or affine copy of y_train (corpus_schema.leak_audit_train, the P5
per-context leak recompute) is refused as target_leak: counted, never labelled.

LINEAR-DETERMINED (owner ruling, owner-decisions-20261006T112914Z.json). The exclusion file
(jope.dl-v2-linear-determined-exclusion/v1, pinned by sha256, a REQUIRED input, loaded by dltrain.exclusions) names
contexts whose target is linear in the train-row features: they are refused as target_linear_in_features (recorded
in refusals.jsonl, never indexed, never in a tranche; they stay S1-scored in the ledger, which is never touched). Its
review contexts are emitted with flags ["target_linear_near_review"] on the context row and index line. OUT/header.json
binds the file's sha256; the manifest and every tranche receipt record its sha256, the excluded count and the
review-flag count; load_tranche re-checks every row against it.

ADMISSION (fail closed). Every context passes dltrain.admission.Admission with the P3 assignment passed explicitly
(raw-v2 view_ids are not in the denylist's assignment_by_view): sealed_excluded and heldout are refused (never
emitted); an unknown assignment, a denylisted view or object key, a P3/input-list/denylist disagreement or any
assignment outside {train_fit, train_nonpool, dev} is refused. dev is emitted with split_role "dev" (selection and
validation only, never train_fit; Admission(purpose="train") must refuse it); train_fit and train_nonpool contexts are
emitted with split_role "train_fit". Refusals are recorded once in refusals.jsonl.

OUTPUT (OUT, one writer under OUT/.lock):
  header.json                  create-only binding (ledger header, input list, P3, denylist, vocab, max_seq, seeds)
  shards/<c2>/<ctx>.json       create-only canonical JSON (SHARD_SCHEMA): context row, train records, refused
                               records, label summary, encoding references; a rebuild with other bytes refuses
  prov/<c2>/<ctx>.json         create-only provenance sidecar (not read by training)
  index.jsonl                  append-only, one canonical line per emitted shard (seq, ids, split_role, sha256)
  refusals.jsonl conflicts.jsonl  append-only
  state.json manifest.json     atomically replaced (cursors and context states; deterministic counts and sha256s)

LOADER API (what the trainer calls):
  read_index(out_dir, offset=0) -> (entries, next_offset)                incremental, complete lines only
  load_shard(out_dir, entry, verify=True) -> dict
  load_corpus_inputs(out_dir, components=DEFAULT_COMPONENTS, split_roles=("train_fit", "dev"), admission=None,
                     on_missing="exclude") -> CorpusInputs(contexts, records, encodings, assignment, skipped)
  build_corpus(out_dir, **kw) -> dltrain.sampler.Corpus                  = Corpus.build(*load_corpus_inputs(...)[:4])
  ShardFollower(out_dir, **kw).poll() -> new entries; .inputs() -> CorpusInputs so far
  tranche_entries(index_path, upto=None) -> [{"context_sha256", "shard_sha256"}]  the first `upto` index lines in
                     arrival order; tranche k-1's list is an exact prefix of tranche k's; train.corpus_manifest_sha256
  load_tranche(out_dir, entries, admission, components=DEFAULT_COMPONENTS, on_missing="raise", exclusion=None)
                     -> (contexts, records, encodings, assignment): entries must be a prefix of the index, every shard
                     file is sha256-checked against its entry, rows pass tranche.admit_contexts and the exclusion
                     (default: the one bound in OUT/header.json) (fail closed)
  tranche_receipt(out_dir, upto=None) / write_tranche_receipt(out_dir, upto=None) -> manifest sha256, counts and
                     the exclusion's sha256 / excluded / review-flag counts (create-only OUT/tranches/*.json)
  data_refs(out_dir, components=DEFAULT_COMPONENTS) -> {vocab_size, vocab_sha256, max_seq, ctx_dim, ...}
  train_data_for_tranche(spec, entries, admission, sampler_config, *, encoding_trees=None, receipt=None);
  grammar_binding(data) -> {path, sha256}; train_data_receipt(data) -> dict
                     -> sampler.TrainData for data-spec kind "s1-tranche/v1" (policylm_main.load_data): load_tranche
                     (on_missing="exclude", never substituted), Corpus.build, sampler.fit, the run's SYNTAX grammar
                     (built once at k=0 and pinned by sha256), per-record SYNTAX masks, refs; `receipt` is filled

ASSUMED FORMATS (observed in s1-prod/code at 2026-10-06T10:41Z and the qualification pulls; adapters below):
  adapt_ledger_row     open{ledger, header_sha256} assign{context_sha256, view_id, use, host} defer/release
                       final{context_sha256, view_id, set, cut_id, use, host, outcome, attempts{charged,by_status},
                       selected_program_sha256, final_sha256, summary_sha256, host_custody}
